Given $a$ and $b$ are positive real numbers, we have two conditions:

1. $\frac{1}{a}+\frac{1}{b}\leq 2\sqrt{2}$
2. $\left(a-b\right)^{2}=4\left(ab\right)^{3}$

From the first condition, we can manipulate the inequality as follows:
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{a}+\frac{1}{b} &\leq 2\sqrt{2} \\
\Rightarrow a+b &\leq 2\sqrt{2}ab \quad \text{(Multiplying both sides by } ab \text{, since } a,b > 0) \\
\Rightarrow \left(a+b\right)^{2} &\leq 8a^{2}b^{2} \quad \text{(Squaring both sides)}
\end{align*}

From the second condition, we have:
\begin{align*}
\left(a-b\right)^{2} &= 4\left(ab\right)^{3} \\
\Rightarrow \left(a+b\right)^{2} &= 4ab\left(a^{2}b^{2}+1\right) \quad \text{(Expanding and rearranging)}
\end{align*}

Combining the inequalities, we get:
\begin{align*}
4ab\left(a^{2}b^{2}+1\right) &\leq 8a^{2}b^{2} \\
\Rightarrow a^{2}b^{2}+1 &\leq 2a^{2}b^{2} \\
\Rightarrow a^{2}b^{2}-2ab+1 &\leq 0 \\
\Rightarrow \left(ab-1\right)^{2} &\leq 0
\end{align*}

Since $\left(ab-1\right)^{2} \leq 0$, and the square of a real number is non-negative, it implies that $\left(ab-1\right)^{2} = 0$, which means $ab=1$. Therefore, $a=\frac{1}{b}$.

To find $\log_{a}b$, we substitute $a=\frac{1}{b}$:
\begin{align*}
\log_{a}b &= \log_{\frac{1}{b}}b \\
&= \log_{b^{-1}}b \\
&= -1 \quad \text{(Using the property } \log_{b^{-1}}b = -\log_{b}b = -1\text{)}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the answer is $\boxed{D}$.

